        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "b9252ea4-3a76-5f39-835d-d0fa58d37150",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: ʻUmi-a-Līloa, High Chief Liloa, Charles Kanaʻina, Hākau, Piʻilani\n- Roles: aliʻi-ai-moku, district high chief\n- Places: Hawaiʻi Island, Maui, Hawaiʻi\n- Concepts: Hawaiian religion, Native Hawaiians, polytheism, animism, deities, spirits\n\nFacts:\n- In the fifteenth century, ʻUmi-a-Līloa was a ruling aliʻi-ai-moku, or district high chief, of Hawaiʻi.\n- ʻUmi-a-Līloa inherited the religious authority of Hawaiʻi from his father, High Chief Liloa.\n- High Chief Liloa's lineage was traced as unbroken to Hawaiian creation.\n- Hawaiian religion encompasses the indigenous religious beliefs and practices of the Native Hawaiians.\n- Hawaiian religion is polytheistic and animistic, with belief in many deities and spirits.\n- Hawaiian religion includes the belief that spirits are present in non-human beings and objects such as animals, waves, and the sky.\n- Charles Kanaʻina was a descendant of Liloa, Hākau, ʻUmi-a-Līloa of Hawaiʻi Island, and Piʻilani of Maui.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "f74a5beb-a288-59bc-969b-cbbb326d4a19",
        "text": "Page: Umi-a-Liloa. Sentence: ʻUmi-a-Līloa (fifteenth century) was a ruling ali'i-ai-moku (district high chief of Hawai'i) who inherited religious authority of Hawai'i from his father, High Chief Liloa, whose line is traced, unbroken to Hawaiian \"creation\".\nPage: Hawaiian religion. Sentence: Hawaiian religion encompasses the indigenous religious beliefs and practices of the Native Hawaiians.\nPage: Hawaiian religion. Sentence: It is polytheistic and animistic, with a belief in many deities and spirits, including the belief that spirits are found in non-human beings and objects such as animals, the waves, and the sky.\nPage: Charles Kanaʻina. Sentence: Kanaʻina was a descendant of several figures from ancient Hawaiian history, including Liloa, Hakau and Umi-a-Liloa of Hawaiʻ i Island as well as Piilani of Maui.",
